  "response": "## Portability sweep: `hardcoded_local_path`\n\n### Inventory\n\n`crimes scan --format json` returns **42 findings, of which exactly one is type `hardcoded_local_path`**:\n\n| Finding | File:line | Literal (verbatim from evidence) | Severity / confidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00018` | `src/api/files.ts:9` | `` `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` @L9 `` | medium / 0.90 |\n\nThat's the whole set. I corroborated it with a raw regex sweep for `/Users/<name>`, `/home/<name>`, and `C:\\Users\\` across the fixture — including `.crimes/suppressions.json`, which the scan reported as excluded and therefore never read (`coverage.warnings[0]`). The grep found the same single hit, so the exclusion didn't hide anything here.\n\n### The one path, and how it should be anchored\n\nCurrent source (`src/api/files.ts:8-15`):\n\n```ts\nconst ADMIN_DASHBOARD = \"http://localhost:3000/admin\";\nconst SCHEMA_PATH = \"/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json\";\n\nexport function loadPlanSchema(): unknown {\n  if (!existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)) return null;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\n**Verdict: relative path resolved from the repo root, with a `process.env` override. Not `os.homedir()`.**\n\nThe finding's own remedy text says \"replace with `os.homedir()`, `process.env.HOME`, or a config-driven base path\" — that's the detector's generic advice, and for this literal the first option is wrong. Read the path: `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json`. The tail is `dev/crimes/schema.json` — the repo's own name followed by a file that lives *inside* the checkout. `os.homedir()` would only reconstruct it as `~/dev/crimes/schema.json`, which is portable in the sense that it compiles everywhere and correct only on machines where every developer happens to clone into `~/dev/crimes`. That trades a hard break (`ENOENT` on line 12, loud) for a soft one (works for whoever matches the author's layout, silently returns `null` for everyone else, because `loadPlanSchema` swallows a missing file). The home directory is not the natural anchor; the checkout is.\n\nProposed replacement:\n\n```ts\nimport { existsSync, readFileSync, statSync } from \"node:fs\";\nimport { dirname, resolve } from \"node:path\";\nimport { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\n\n// schema.json ships with the repo; resolve it relative to this module,\n// not to whoever's machine last edited the constant.\nconst REPO_ROOT = resolve(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), \"../..\");\nconst SCHEMA_PATH = process.env.PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH ?? resolve(REPO_ROOT, \"schema.json\");\n```\n\n(CJS equivalent: `resolve(__dirname, \"../..\")`.) The `process.env.PLAN_SCHEMA_PATH` escape hatch is there for the deploy case where the schema is baked into an image at a path unrelated to the source tree — but it's the override, not the primary. Repo-relative is the default because a repo-relative file should resolve without any environment configuration on a fresh clone.\n\nTwo follow-ons worth doing in the same change, since the failure mode is what makes this path dangerous rather than merely ugly:\n\n1. `loadPlanSchema` returns `null` for \"file missing\" and \"file empty\" alike (lines 12–14). A schema that ships with the repo being absent is a bug, not a state — throw on missing rather than returning `null`, so a bad anchor surfaces at the first call instead of downstream.\n2. Add a test that asserts `existsSync(SCHEMA_PATH)`. That's what stops the literal regressing; a portability fix with no test just moves the machine-specific assumption from a string into a `resolve()` call.\n\n### Adjacent, but not `hardcoded_local_path`\n\n`src/api/files.ts:8` also carries `crime_00017` (`hardcoded_localhost`, medium/0.90), evidence `` `localhost:3000` @L8 ``. It's a different detector and a different fix — a URL, not a filesystem path, so neither `os.homedir()` nor repo-relative resolution applies. It belongs to the same sweep in the sense that both literals are one developer's machine leaking into source, and both live in the same six-line block; the fix is `process.env.ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL` with the dev value in `.env.example`. Flagging it so the sweep doesn't touch line 9 and leave line 8, but it is not one of the paths you asked me to enumerate.\n\n### Scope note\n\nOne finding is a thin sweep. The scan covered 21 files in the `01-messy-ts-app` fixture; if the intent was a portability sweep across the real `crimes` monorepo rather than this fixture, point me at that root and I'll re-run — the plan shape above (repo-relative default, env override, existence test) carries over, but the inventory would not.",
